ke was powdering Miss Blacklock's face in the bathroom, wasn't she?\" [2301] \"Chin! You think it was her? \u2014\"\n[2302] \"I don't know. If she did, it would be too obvious. If you wanted to put the pills there, I don't think you'd want someone to see you in the bathroom, would you? There are plenty of other opportunities.\" [2303] \"The men didn't go upstairs.\" [2304] \"There's the back staircase. Besides, if a man left the house, you wouldn't follow him around to make sure he really went where you did, would you? That would be undignified! Anyway, don't argue with me, Miss Mulgrew. I want to start over from the first murder of Letty Blacklock. Now, first of all, remember the facts, because it all depends on you.\" [2305] Miss Mulgrew looked tense. [2306] \"Oh, dear, Chippy, you know I'm all mixed up about all this! -\"\n[2307] \"The problem is not with your brain, or with the gray cells you call your brain. The problem is with your eyes; the problem is what you saw at the time. -\"\n[2308] \"But I saw nothing. -\"\n[2309] \"As I said, your trouble is, Murgatroyd, you don't try hard enough. Now, here's what happened that night: whoever went to kill Letty Blacklock that night was in the house. He - I say he, because it's more convenient to say, but there's no reason to assume it was a man and not a woman, except that men are generally scoundrels - anyway, he oiled the door from the living room to the outside, which should have been nailed shut. Don't ask me when he did it, because that would muddy the waters. In fact, if I had to pick a time, I could walk into any pub in Chipping Clegg-horn and do anything I wanted in about half an hour without anyone noticing. Find out where the staff is, when the owner goes out, where exactly he's going, and for how long. Now, let's think, he oiled the second door so that it wouldn't make a sound when opened. The plan was: lights out, door A - the front door - opened with a bang. Flash a flashlight and say the word \"robbery\"