o the fireplace in the small parlor, I don't know what she went there for, because just then the lights went out. [2322] \"That's how everyone was positioned: Patrick Simmons was in the living room, Philippa Hemmes was there, and either Eastbrook Colonel or Edmund Swetnam - but I don't know who. Now, Muggleton, pay attention, the most likely culprit is one of these three. Anyone who wanted to get out of the door from a distance would have to be in a convenient position to act as soon as the lights went out. So I say the most likely culprit is one of these three. If that's the case, Muggleton, you're out of luck!\" [2323] It was clear that Miss Muggleton's face lit up. [2324] \"On the other hand,\" Miss Chinchcliffe continued, \"it may not be any of the three. That's where you come in, Muggleton.\" [2325] \"But how could I know the situation at the time?\" [2326] \"I've already said that if you don't know, then no one does.\" [2327] \"But I don't know! I really don't know! I couldn't see anything!\" [2328] \"Oh, no, you could see. You were the only one who could see. You were standing behind the door, and you couldn't look at the flashlight because the door was between you and the flashlight. You were facing the other way, in the same direction as the flashlight. The rest of us were blinded by the flashlight, but you weren't.\" [2329] \"Yes, yes, maybe so, but I couldn't see anything, the flashlight was flashing around.\" [2330] \"What did it illuminate? Was it shining on everyone's faces? On the table? On the chairs?\" [2331] \"Yes, yes, that's right... Miss Bonner, she had her mouth wide open, her eyes bulging out, blinking in terror.\" [2332] \"That's right! It was hard to get you to use your gray cells, Miss Quincannon. So, what happened next?\" [2333] \"But I didn't see anything else, really.\" [2334] \"So, you saw an empty room? With no one standing or sitting?\" [2335] \"No, of course not. Miss Bonner had her eyes wide open, and Mrs. Harmon was sitting on the arm of a chair, with her eyes closed tightly and her hands covering her face \u2013 like a child.\" [2336] \"Very well, these are Mrs. Harmon and Miss Bonner. Do you still not understand what I'm getting at? The difficult part is that I don't want to put my thoughts into your head. But once we eliminate the people you have seen, we can get to the point, which is whether there are any people you haven't seen. Understood? Besides the table, chair, chrysanthemums, and so on, there are still some people left: Julia Simmons, Mrs. Swetnam, Mrs. Eastbrook \u2013 either Colonel Eastbrook or Mr. Edmund Swetnam, Miss Dora Bonner, and Round-harmon. Cross them off one by one. Now, think, Mr. Mulgrew, think carefully, are there any who were not present at the time?\" [2337] A branch hung onto the open window, and Miss Muggleton jumped slightly in fright. She closed her eyes and muttered to herself\u2026\n[2338] \"The flowers on the table... the big armchair... the flashlight's beam hasn't reached you yet, Mrs. Harmon, yes...\". [2339] The telephone rang urgently. Miss Quincy walked up to the telephone. [2340] \"Hello, yes. Police station?\" [2341] Miss Muggeridge closed her eyes tightly and relived the scene from the night of the 29th. Flashlight, slowly scanning... a group of people... the window... the sofa... Dora Bonner... the walls... the table with the lamp... the arcade... the revolver suddenly firing...\n[2342] \"...that's quite unusual!\" said Miss Muggeridge. [2343] \"What?\" Miss Chinchcliffe shouted into the phone, angry. \"Has it been there since this morning? When?\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nasked Miss Blacklock. [417] \"Well, I'm afraid he's dead\u2026 probably suicide\u2014or he tripped over that cloak-like thing and fell, and the revolver went off. If I could see more clearly\u2026\"\n[418] Just then, as if by magic, all the lights came on. [419] With a strange sense of unreality, the oddly assorted occupants of the village of Ockham's Cross stood in the hallway of the small courtyard, aware that they were in the midst of a scene of violence and sudden death. Colonel Eastbrooke's hand was stained with blood, which was still trickling down Miss Blacklock's neck onto her blouse and overcoat, and the intruder's sinister body lay at their feet\u2026\n[420] Patrick came in from the dining room and said, \"It seems there's only one fuse missing\u2026\" He broke off. Colonel Eastbrooke reached out a hand towards the small black mask. [421] \"Best see who this fellow is,\" he said. \"But I don't think it's anyone we know...\"\n[422] He took off the mask. Several necks craned forward. Midge gave a stifled cry and drew in her breath, but the others were silent. [423] \"He's young,\" said Mrs. Harmon without sympathy. [424] Suddenly, Dora Bunner exclaimed:\n[425] \"Lettie, Lettie, it's the young man from the Medenham Club. The one who came to ask you for money to go back to Switzerland and you refused him. I think he came here just to spy out the house... Oh, dear, he could have killed you so easily...\"\n[426] At this moment, Miss Blacklock was as calm as a commander. She issued sharp orders: \"Philip, take Bonnie to the dining room and give her a glass of brandy. Julia dear, go to the medicine cabinet in the bathroom and get some adhesive tape. Quickly, there's blood everywhere, like a slaughterhouse. Patrick, can you call the police station and ask them to send someone over right away?\" [427] Chapter 4: The Search for the Hotel\n[428] 1\n[429] George Ridestad, the Chief Constable of Middlesex, was a man of few words, of medium height, with shrewd, piercing eyes under bushy eyebrows. He was accustomed to listening rather than talking, and then, with a voice devoid of emotion, issuing a concise command - and that command was always obeyed by his subordinates. [430] He was currently listening to Inspector Delmont Corrado's report. Corrado had officially taken charge of the case at Little Wraps, and Ridestad had summoned him back last night. Ridestad had high regard for Corrado, considering him to be a man of brains and imagination. What he appreciated even more was Corrado's strict self-discipline and steady demeanor, always double-checking every fact and keeping an open mind until the case was closed. [431] \"Inspector Legg took the call, sir,\" Corrado said. \"He seemed to handle it well, both decisively and wisely. It must have been a difficult situation, with about ten people all talking at once, including one from Central Europe. She was convinced that she would be locked up and was almost screaming to the point of collapsing the place.\" [432] \"The identity of the deceased has been confirmed?\" [433] \"Yes, sir. Rudolf Scherz, Swiss nationality. He worked as a receptionist at the Royal Spa Hotel in Medenham.\" [434] \"If you agree, sir, I will first go to the Royal Spa Hotel and then to Chipping Craighorn. Inspector Fletcher is already on the scene, and he will first see the people on the bus and then go to the mansion.\" [435] Ridestad nodded in agreement. The door opened, and the chief inspector looked up. [436] \"Come in, Henry,\" he said. \"We've had an unusual incident here.\" [437] Sir Henry Clersalin - former Commissioner of the Metropolitan Police - stepped into the room, frowning slightly. He was a tall, distinguished-looking man. [438] \"This may interest your tired palate,\" Ridestadle continued. [439] \"I've never been tired of it,\" Sir Henry grumbled. [440] \"The latest trick,\" Ridestadle said, \"is to advertise the murder before committing it. Show Henry the notice, Corridoke.\" [441] \"The 'Barnham News and Chipping Cleghorn Gazette',\" Sir Henry said. \"Splendid. He looked at the half-inch square of printed text pointed out by Cordeaux. [442] \"Ah, yes, quite unusual.\" [443] \"Any clues as to who placed the ad?\" Ridderdale asked. [444] \"According to the description, it was sent by Rudi Schlesinger himself - on Tuesday,\" Cordeaux replied. [445] \"No one questioned it? The recipient didn't find it odd?\" [446] \"The blonde girl who accepted the advertisement had a thyroid enlargement, I must say, sir, she wasn't very bright. She checked the word count and took the money.\" [447] \"What's this all about?\" asked Sir Henry. [448] \"It would pique the curiosity of many locals,\u201d Ridesdale speculated, \u201cto get them to a specific location at a specific time, and then detain them, searching them for cash and valuables. As an idea, it's not without its merits.\" [449] \"What's Chipping Craighorn like?\" asked Sir Henry. [450] \"A picturesque town, spread haphazardly. There's a butcher, a baker, a general store, and a rather nice antique shop, as well as two tea shops. It's a charming spot, suitable for both day trips and living. Former agricultural worker's cottages have been renovated and now house elderly spinsters and retired couples. Additionally, there are some Victorian-era buildings.\" [451] \"Well-bred old maids and retired majors,\" Sir Henry said, \"I see. Yes, if they saw that advertisement, they would all arrive at 6:30 to find out what's going on. Good heavens, if my particular old maid were here, she would certainly get involved, it would be right up her alley.\" [452] \"Who is your particular old maid, Henry, a cousin?\" [453] \"No,\" Sir Henry sighed, \"not a relative. She's just the best detective God ever created. A self-grown genius in the right soil.\" [454] He turned to Cordeaux. [455] \"Don't underestimate the old maid in this village, my child,\" he said, \"it could be a baffling case. I don't mean it has to be. But remember, the unmarried spinster who knits and gardens is far more astute than any police chief. She can tell you what might have happened, what should have happened, even what actually happened! And why it happened!\" [456] \"I will remember that, sir. \" Corrado Inspector answered very seriously, but no one would guess that Delmon Eric Corrado was actually Sir Henry's godson, and in a close, intimate relationship with his godfather. [457] Laidstall briefly gave his friend a summary of the case. [458] \"They all showed up at a quarter to six, that's a fact. \" He said. \"Did the Swissman know they would be there? And could they have brought a lot of cash and valuables to be stolen?\" [459] \"A couple of old-fashioned brooches, a few strands of small pearls \u2013 a little change, perhaps a couple of banknotes \u2013 that's all. \" Sir Henry mused. \"Does Miss Blacklock have a lot of money at home?\" [460] \"She said no, sir. Only five pounds in cash as far as I know. \" [461] \"Just chicken feed,\" Reddish said. [462] \"You mean,\" Sir Henry said, \"this guy likes to put on a show - not a robbery at all, just a prank, pretending to rob. Like in a movie? Well, quite possible. How did he shoot himself?\" [463] Reddish handed him a piece of paper. [464] \"According to the preliminary report from the forensic team, the revolver was fired at close range - the skin was burned. He... cannot prove whether it was an accident or suicide. It could have been intentional, or he could have tripped and fallen, and the revolver in his hand went off accidentally... I would say the latter.\" [465] \"They all saw something different,\" Detective Inspector Corradino said, disappointed. [466] \"This has always been interesting to me,\" said Henry Sir, \"what people really see in moments of extreme excitement and extreme nervousness. What they actually saw, and even more intriguing, what they didn't see.\" [467] \"Where is the report on the revolver?\" [468] \"Foreign-made-quite common on the continent of Europe-which Shells had no gun license and did not declare upon entering Britain.\" [469] \"Bad boy,\" commented Henry Sir. [470] \"There are a lot of unsatisfied people around. Well, Corrado, go to the Royal Spa Resort Hotel and see if you can find out anything about him.\" [471] 2\n[472] Upon arriving at the Royal Spa Hydro, Inspector Corrigan was immediately escorted to the manager's office. [473] The manager, Mr. Rowlandson, was tall and ruddy-faced, with a cordial manner. He received Inspector Corrigan with great warmth. [474] \"I'm glad to be of any help I can,\" he said. \"It's a shocking business. I don't approve of such things \u2013 not at all. Sherris seemed like a very pleasant, ordinary young man \u2013 I never thought he'd be up to anything like this.\" [475] \"How long did he work for you, Mr. Rowlandson?\" [476]\"I was just reviewing his records. About three months. Excellent recommendation letters, all the necessary permits, etc.\" [477]\"Are you satisfied with him?\" [478]Before Mr. Rollason could answer, Inspector Corradino noticed a slight but unintentional pause. [479]\"Quite satisfied.\" [480]Inspector Corradino employed one of his most effective tactics. [481] \"No, no, Mr. Rollins,\" he said, shaking his head slowly, \"it's not like that, is it?\" [482] \"Uh\u2014\" the manager was somewhat taken aback. [483] \"Well, what's the matter? What's not right?\" [484] \"There's something not right, but I don't know what it is.\" [485] \"But you feel something's not right?\" [486] \"Ah \u2014 yes \u2014 I've thought about it... but there's no real evidence. I don't want my suspicions recorded and used against me.\" [487] Koller smiled pleasantly. [488] \"I understand what you mean. You don't have to worry. But we need to know what kind of person Scherz is. What have you suspected about him?\" [489] Reluctantly, Rolandsen spoke:\n[490] \"Well, there were a couple of incidents, regarding the bills. There were items that shouldn't have been charged.\" [491] \"You mean you suspect him of charging certain fees that don't exist in the hotel's records and then pocketing the difference after the guests pay their bills?\u201d\n[492] \"Something like that... I guess he's just careless. There were a couple of instances where the amounts were quite significant. To be honest, I had the accountant check his books and suspected him... uh... of faking them. But despite all the errors, most of the accounts were reported fairly, and the actual cash balance was correct. So, I must have made a mistake myself.\" [493] \"What if you're not mistaken? If Sherz is skimming a little off the top in every place, wouldn't he use his own money to make up for the difference?\" [494] \"If he had that kind of money, then yes. But people who skim usually end up with very little money in their pockets.\" [495] \"So, if he needs money to make up for the difference, he would have to get it somehow \u2013 either by robbing or through some other means?\" [496] \"That's right. I was wondering if this was his first time doing it...\"\n[497] \"Maybe, it was pretty clumsy. Could he get money from anyone else? Does he have a woman in his life?\" [498] \"There's a waitress at the steakhouse named Mona Harris.\" [499] \"I should talk to her.\" [500] 3\n[501] Mona Harris is a pretty girl with bright red hair and a prominent nose. [502] She is cautious and alert, fearing that answering questions from the police could damage her reputation. [503] \"I don't know anything about this, sir. Not at all. If I had known that Rudy was like this, I would never have gone out with him. Of course, seeing him working at the front desk, I thought he was nice. I mean, the restaurant hires people \u2013 especially foreigners \u2013 they should be more careful. Because dealing with foreigners, you never know what they're like. I guess he's a member of one of the gangs you've listed?\" [504] \"We think,\" Corradino said, \"that he's working alone.\" [505] \"Strange \u2013 he was so quiet and respectable. Who would have thought? Although I have lost things \u2013 I remember now, a diamond brooch and a gold coin collection box. I'm sure it was. But I never would have thought it was Rudy.\" [506] \"I'm sure you wouldn't have thought so,\" said Colado. \"People get taken in. Are you close to him?\" [507] \"I don't know if I can say we're close.\" [508] \"But you liked each other?\" [509] \"Oh, we got along well \u2013 that's all. Not seriously, of course, I'm always very cautious with foreigners. They always have their own agendas. You can never tell who they are, can you? Some are Polish refugees from the war! Some are even Americans! They never mention they're married until it's too late. Rudi talks big \u2013 but I always take it with a grain of salt.\" [510] Colado seized on the word. [511] \"He talks big, does he? That's very interesting, Miss Harris. I can see that you'll be of great help to us. In what way does he exaggerate?\" [512] \"For example, how rich and prominent his family is in Switzerland\u2014how he can't bring the money here due to financial regulations. It's possible, I suppose. But his clothes aren't expensive. I mean, they're not up to par. And I remember, he used to tell me all these incredible stories about climbing the Alps and saving people's lives on glacier cliffs. But just walking the ridge of Mount B\u00fcrgerli made him dizzy. Pah, the Alps!\u201d\n[513] \"Do you spend a lot of time with him?\" [514] \"Yes\u2014uh\u2014yes. He's quite a gentleman, and he knows how to\u2014how to take care of a lady. He always chooses the best seats at the movies, and sometimes even buys me flowers. And his dancing is superb\u2014really superb.\" [515] \"Has he mentioned Miss Blacklock to you?\" [516] \"She sometimes comes here for lunch, doesn't she? She stayed here once. No, I don't think Rudi has ever mentioned her. I don't know if he knows her.\" [517] \"Has he mentioned Chipping Clegg-horn?\" [518] Mr. Corraduccio noticed a slight anxiety in Miss Harris's eyes, but he couldn't be sure. [519] \"I think not... I believe he did ask about the bus schedule once - about the timings - but I can't remember if it was going to Chipping Clegg-horn or somewhere else. That was some time ago.\" [520] From her, he couldn't get any more information. Rudi Scherz seemed ordinary. He hadn't seen him the night before last. He didn't know - not at all - he emphasized this - Rudi Scherz was a fraud. [521] Perhaps, thought Corradoch, that was the truth. [522] Chapter V: The Two Misses\n[523] 1\n[524] The small estate was exactly as Corradoch Inspector imagined. He noticed the ducks, the chickens, and a once-beautiful flowerbed with purple asters in their last bloom. The lawn and paths looked neglected. [525] \"All in all,\" Corradoch Inspector thought, \"this household probably doesn't have much money to hire a gardener, but they like flowers and have a knack for arranging them. The house needs a paint job, and most houses do these days. The small property is still in decent shape.\" [526] Corrado's car pulled up to the front door, and Chief Constable Fletcher emerged from the side of the house. He stood like a sentry, with a straight back and a military bearing, adept at conveying multiple meanings with a single word. [527] \"Sir.\" [528] \"Ah, Fletcher. There you are.\" [529] \"Sir.\" Chief Constable Fletcher replied. [530] \"Anything to report?\" [531] \"We've searched the whole house, sir. Sherris seems to have left no fingerprints anywhere. Of course, he was wearing gloves. There were no signs of forced entry through the doors or windows. He appears to have come by public bus from Medenham, arriving here at six o'clock. I found out that the side door was locked at five-thirty. It seems he must have come through the front door. Miss Blacklock stated that that door is usually locked only after everyone is asleep. On the other hand, the maid claimed that the front door was locked all afternoon\u2014though she's notoriously unreliable. You'll find her quite difficult to deal with. These Central European refugees.\" [532] \"She can be quite a handful, can't she?\" [533] \"Sir!\" Chief Inspector Fletcher exclaimed. [534] Colard\u00f3k smiled. [535] The Chief Inspector continued his report: \"The lighting system is in order throughout, and we haven't yet discovered how he manipulated the lighting. At the time, only one circuit was broken, in the living room and hallway. Of course, today's wall lamps and chandeliers don't rely on the same fuse, but the wiring and installation here are quite old-fashioned. We don't know how he tampered with the fuse box, as it's located in the pantry, and he would have had to pass through the kitchen, where the maid would have seen him. [536] \"Unless they were both inside at the time?\" [537] \"That's quite possible. Both being foreigners, and I don't trust her\u2014not at all.\" [538] Corrado noticed two frightened and large eyes peering out from behind the window of the front door. The face was flattened by the pressed glass, making it almost indiscernible. [539] \"Is that her?\" [540] \"Yes, sir.\" [541] The face disappeared. [542] Kolarik rang the doorbell at the front door. [543] After a long wait, the door was opened by a young, attractive woman with a mane of chestnut hair and a bored expression. [544] \"Kolarik Inspector.\" Kolarik said. [545] The young woman looked at him with her seductive, light brown eyes and said:\n[546] \"Come in.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nMiss Blacklock is waiting for you.\" [547] Colardoke noticed that the hallway was long and seemed to have doors everywhere. [548] The young woman pushed open the door on the left and said, \"Colardoke Inspector has arrived, Aunt Letty. Mickey didn't want to open the door, she locked herself in her room and made some strange moaning sounds. I don't think we'll be having any lunch.\" [549] \"She doesn't like the police,\" she added in an explanatory tone to Colardoke. Then she went out and closed the door behind her. [550] Colardoke approached the hostess of the small estate. [551] He saw a tall, well-preserved woman of about sixty years. Her gray hair was naturally wavy, with an elegant hairstyle that highlighted her intelligent, determined face. Her gray eyes were piercing, and her jawline was square and resolute, with a bandage wrapped around her left ear. She was not wearing makeup, only a well-tailored tweed suit, skirt, and sweater. Over the sweater's collar, she wore an unexpected string of antique jade carvings \u2013 a subtle Victorian sentiment. [552] Standing beside her was a woman of similar age, with a round face and an anxious expression. Her hair was disheveled and escaping from a hairnet. Corradino had no difficulty recognizing her as \"Dora Bunner \u2013 companion,\" as mentioned in the report by Inspector Legge. Legge had added an unofficial comment about her: \"Inept!\" [553] Miss Blacklock spoke in a pleasant, well-bred tone. [554] \"Good morning, Detective Corradino. This is my friend Miss Bunner, who helps me with the household. Please sit down. You don't smoke, I presume?\" [555] \"I'm afraid not, Miss Blacklock, during duty hours.\" [556] \"How unfortunate!\" [557] Corraduccio quickly and carefully surveyed the room. A typical Victorian double living room. One had two long windows, and the other had a bay window... chairs... sofas... a table in the middle with a large vase of chrysanthemums - another on the windowsill - all fresh and pretty, but lacking novelty. The only thing that didn't fit the whole scene was a silver vase with wilted violets. It was placed on a table next to the archway leading to the inner room. It was hard to imagine Miss Blacklock tolerating dead flowers in her well-maintained house, and in Corraduccio's opinion, if anything out of the ordinary had happened in this house, this was the only clue. [558] He spoke up: \"I assume the accident happened in this room, Miss Blacklock?\" [559] \"Indeed.\" [560] \"You should have seen it last night,\" Miss Bonna exclaimed excitedly, \"it was a mess. Two small tables were knocked over, one of the legs of one broke - everyone bumping into each other - and someone dropped a lit cigarette, burning one of the best pieces of furniture. Those people - especially the young ones - don't care about these things... fortunately, no ceramics were broken...\". [561] Miss Blacklock interrupted her kindly but firmly. [562] \"Dora, all this may be annoying, but it's just trivial matters. I think it's best to answer Inspector Kolladok's questions.\" [563] \"Thank you, Miss Blacklock. I'll ask about what happened last night right away. First, I'd like to know when was the last time you saw the deceased - Rudi Scherz?\" [564] \"Rudi Scherz?\" Miss Blacklock looked slightly surprised. \"I vaguely remember... never mind, it's irrelevant. The first time I met him was when I went to the Medenham Pleasure Gardens to buy something, about three weeks ago. We - Miss Bonner and I - had lunch at the Royal Spa Hotel. After lunch, we were about to leave when I heard someone call my name, it was this young man. He said, 'Are you Miss Blacklock, isn't it?' Then he said I probably didn't remember him, he was the son of the owner of the Alpine Hotel in Monte Carlo, where my sister and I stayed for almost a year during the war.\" [565] \"The Alpine Hotel in Monte Carlo,\" Kolladok repeated, \"Do you remember him, Miss Blacklock?\" [566] \"No, I don't. In fact, I can't recall ever seeing him before. The front desk clerks at hotels all look alike. My sister and I had a wonderful time in Monte Carlo, and the hotel owner was very helpful, so I was as polite as I could be to him and wished him well in England. He said that his father had sent him here for six months to learn hotel management. It all seemed perfectly natural.\" [567] \"What about the next encounter?\" [568] \"About ten days ago, he suddenly appeared here. I was very surprised to see him. He apologized