e! I wondered if they would return the missing pillowcase. I must make a note of it in my book. I'll take care of it right now.\" [633] \"Take these violets away, too,\" said Miss Blacklock. \"I hate dead flowers.\" [634] \"It's a shame. I just picked them yesterday. They didn't last long - oh, dear, I must have forgotten to add water to the vase. How could I forget? Now I must go and see about the laundry people. They could arrive any minute.\" [635] She brightened up and hurried out, looking relieved. [636] \"Her will isn't very strong,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"Excitement is bad for her. Is there anything else you need to know, Inspector?\" [637] \"I just want to know exactly how many people are in your house and something about them.\" [638] \"Sure, besides me and Dora, there are now two young cousins living here, Patrick and Julia Simmons.\" [639] \"Cousins? Not nephews and nieces?\" [640] \"No. Although they call me Aunt, they are actually distant cousins. Their mother is my second cousin.\" [641] \"They've been living here all the time?\" [642] \"Oh, no, only for the last two months. They were living in the south of France before the war. Patrick joined the navy, and I believe Julia was working in a government department. She was in Land's End. After the war, her mother wrote to me asking if they could come here as paying guests\u2014Julia was training as a pharmacist at Milchester General Hospital, and Patrick was studying for an engineering degree at Milchester University. Milchester, you know, is only fifty miles by bus, so I was glad to have them. The house is too big for me. They pay a small sum for board and lodging, and everything has gone well. She smiled and added, \"I like having young people around.\" [643] \"And then there's Mrs. Hyems, I believe?\" [644] \"Yes. She works as a helper in the garden at Deyce House\u2014that's Lucas's house\u2014but the little cottage there is occupied by an old couple, so Lucas asked me if I could find her a place to