 [813] \"Well, no. I thought it was a joke. I was tired and didn't feel like raising my hands.\" [814] \"So, you found the whole thing boring?\" [815] \"Yes, I did. But then the gun went off. The sound was deafening, and I was terrified. The flashlight was waving around, and then it fell to the ground and went out. Then, Micky started screaming, like a pig being slaughtered.\" [816] \"Did you find the flashlight light too bright?\" [817] \"No, not really. It was pretty strong, but it just shone on Miss Bonner for a while. You know, she looked like a turnip lamp [2]. She was really pale, with her mouth wide open and her eyes almost popping out of her head.\" [818] \"Did the man move the flashlight?\" [819] \"Yes, he was shining it all around the room.\" [820] \"As if he was looking for someone?\" [821] \"I don't think so.\" [822] \"What happened next, Mrs. Hyde?\" [823] Philippa Hyde frowned. [824] \"Oh, it was a complete mess. Edmund Swetnam and Patrick Simmons lit their lighters, and the two of them went out to the hallway, with us following behind. Someone opened the door to the dining room \u2013 the fuse hadn't blown, and the lights were still on \u2013 and Edmund Swetnam slapped Midge hard, silencing her scream. After that, things weren't so bad.\" [825] \"Did you see the body?\" [826] \"Yes.\" [827] \"Do you know him? Have you ever met him before?\" [828] \"Never.\" [829] \"Do you think his death was accidental or a suicide?\" [830] \"I have no idea.\" [831] \"Have you ever seen him come to the manor before?\" [832] \"No. I believe he must have come in the morning, when I wasn't here. I'm not home during the day.\" [833] \"Thank you, Mrs. Hammers. Do you have any valuable jewelry? Rings, bracelets, things like that?\" [834] Phyllida shakes her head. [835] \"My engagement ring \u2013 a couple of brooches.\" [836] \"Also, do you know of anything particularly valuable in the manor?\" [837] \"No. I mean, there are some quite decent silverware, but nothing particularly unusual.\" [838] \"Thank you, Mrs. Hedmes.\" [839] 2\n[840] Korduk returns along the same path, and in the garden, he collides with a tall, rosy-cheeked lady with a tightly laced corset. [841] \"Good morning,\" she snapped, \"What are you doing here?\" [842] \"Mrs. Lucas? I'm Inspector Calderon.\" [843] \"Oh, I see. My apologies. I don't like strangers wandering around the garden and wasting the gardener's time. But I understand you're on official business.\" [844] \"Indeed.\" [845] \"Can I ask, will the violence that occurred at Miss Blacklock's last night happen again? Was it a gang-related incident?\" [846] \"We are relieved to inform you, Mrs. Lucas, that it was not the work of a gang.\" [847] \"There are so many robberies these days, the police have become lax.\" [848] \"I need her statement as a witness.\" [849] \"I don't suppose you can wait until 1 pm to question her? It would be fairer to ask her directly rather than using my time...\u201d\n[850] \"I need to return to headquarters urgently.\" [851] \"These days, no one can expect sympathy or to work a full day without any interruptions. Arriving late, taking a half-hour break at ten o'clock, having tea at eleven, and leaving as soon as it starts to rain. When it's time to mow the lawn, the lawnmower breaks down. With five to ten minutes left before the end of the day, she's nowhere to be found.\" [852] \"Mrs. Hedman told me that she left at the usual time yesterday, but worked until five twenty.\" [853] \"Oh, she would say that. Mrs. Hedman is quite dedicated to her work, despite a few days when I couldn't find her anywhere. She was born a lady, of course, and everyone feels responsible for helping these poor widows. Because of the war, they were left alone so young. It's not easy, but there are long breaks at school and arrangements made for that, which means she gets extra time off. I told her about the summer camps, which are really great, and she can send her children there to play and have fun. They don't need to come home during the summer holidays.\" [854] \"But Mrs. Hedman didn't like the suggestion?\" [855] \"That girl is as stubborn as a mule. Just last year, I had the grass cut on the tennis court and the lines painted every day. But Old Ash painted the lines crooked. No one thought about whether it was convenient for me!\" [856] \"I guess Mrs. Hemmings's wages are lower than most people's?\" [857] \"Of course. What else would she expect?\" [858] \"I'm sure she doesn't ask for anything else,\" said Colquhoun. \"Have a nice morning, Mrs. Lucas.\" [859] 3\n[860] \"It's terrible,\" said Miss Sweetnam with a smile. [861] \"Quite\u2014quite\u2014horrible. I mean, the News office should have been more careful when accepting advertisements. I found the notice very suspicious at the time, didn't I, Edmund?\" [862] \"Do you remember what you were doing when the lights went out, Mrs. Swetnam?\" [863] \"It brought back memories of my old nurse. Where was Moses when the light went out? The answer, of course, is 'in the dark.' We were all there last night, wondering what would happen. Then, as you know, there was that terrifying feeling when everything went black. The door opened\u2014there was just a vague figure standing there, a revolver, a blinding light, and a threatening voice saying, 'Money or your life!'\" [864] \"Ah, I've never been so happy. Then, about a minute later, it was absolutely dreadful, a real bullet whizzing past our ears! It was like a charge of a storming party in wartime.\" [865] \"Where were you standing or sitting, Mrs. Swetnam?\" [866] \"Let me think, I was\u2014I was talking to who, Edmund?\" [867] \"I have no idea, Mom.\" [868] \"I was asking Miss Chinchcliff about giving the chickens fish liver oil on cold days, or was it Mrs. Harmon\u2014no, she hadn't arrived yet. I think I was talking to Colonel Eastbrook, and I thought it was very dangerous to build an atomic bomb research station in England. It should be built on a deserted island