l told him. \"You have to understand the criminal. For someone with much more experience than me, the entire trap is obvious. Why did he place the advertisement? Psychological reasons. He wanted to promote himself \u2013 to get everyone's attention. He had been ignored, perhaps the hotel staff looked down on him because he was a foreigner, or maybe a girl had rejected him. Nowadays, what kind of idols are in movies \u2013 gangsters \u2013 tough guys? Great, so he decided to be a tough guy. Violent robbery. A mask? A revolver? But he still needs an audience \u2013 he must have an audience. So he arranges an audience. And then, the moment of climax, his character loses control \u2013 he's not just a thief, but a murderer. He fires \u2013 aimlessly \u2013\"\n[891] Inspector Corrado interrupted at this point. \"You said 'aimlessly,' Colonel Eastbrook. So you think he didn't aim at a specific target \u2013 say, Miss Blacklock?\" [892] \"No, not at all. He just slipped, as I said, aimlessly. That's what got him into trouble. The bullet hit someone \u2013 actually just grazed them, but he didn't know that. He suddenly realized that all this \u2013 these tricks he was playing \u2013 were real. He fired the revolver \u2013 possibly killed someone \u2013 and that pushed him over the edge. So, in a blind panic, he turned the revolver on himself.\" [893] Colonel Eastbrook paused, cleared his throat with satisfaction, and continued triumphantly, \"Clear as day, that's what happened, clear as day.\" [894] \"It's all so interesting,\" said Colonel Eastbrook's wife. \"You know the ins and outs of the whole thing, Archibald.\" [895] Her voice was warm with admiration. [896] Chief Inspector Coradino also found this explanation impressive, but he did not express his approval enthusiastically. [897] \"Where were you in the room, Colonel Eastbrook, when the gun was fired?\" [898] \"My wife and I were standing by the table with the flowers in the middle.\" [899] \"I held your arm, didn't I, Archie? I was absolutely terrified, so I had to hold you.\" [900] \"Poor kitten,\" the colonel jested. [901] 5\n[902] Inspector Corradino had a hard time finding Miss Chickering in the pig pen. [903] \"Pigs are nice animals,\" Miss Chickering said, scratching the pink wrinkled back of the pig, \"they grow up nicely, won't they? By Christmas, they'll be ready to make excellent bacon. Now, what can I do for you? I've told your men that I have no idea who the man was last night. I've never seen him wandering or loitering around here. Our Mrs. Mop says he came from a big hotel in Medenham. Why didn't he just rob him on the road? He could have gotten more that way.\" [904] This was undeniable - Inspector Corradino began the questioning. [905] \"Where were you exactly when the incident occurred?\" [906]\u201cIncident! That reminds me of the days of the air raids. I can tell you I've seen my share of incidents. Where was I when the shooting started? You want to know that, do you?\u201d\n[907]\u201cYes.\u201d\n[908]\u201cRight up against the fireplace, praying to God for a drink.\u201d Miss Chickiff answered without hesitation. [909]\u201cDo you think the bullet was fired randomly or intentionally at someone?\u201d\n[910]\u201cYou mean at Letty Blacklock? Hell, how would I know? It's hard to recall the exact impressions or what really happened after all this. I just know all the lights went out, and a flashlight was shone in our faces, making us dizzy. Then someone fired a shot, and I thought, 'If that damn fool Patrick Simmons had loaded his revolver and was playing around with it, someone would get hurt. '\u201d\n[911] \"You thought it was Patrick Simmons at the time?\" [912] \"Well, it could be. Edmund Swetnam is sensible and writes books, he wouldn't play pranks. Colonel Eastbrooke wouldn't find this amusing either. But Patrick is a wild child. However, I should apologize for this thought.\" [913] \"Did your friend also think it could be Patrick?\" [914] \"Muggleton? You'd better ask her yourself. You won't get much out of her. She's in the orchard. I'll call her over if you like.\" [915] Miss Chickering raised her voice and shouted, \"Hey! Call you, Muggleton...\"\n[916] \"Coming...\" came a faint reply. [917] \"Come here quickly - it's the police.\" [918] Miss Muggleton rushed up, breathless. She had let down her skirts and her hair had slipped out of her too-small net. Her round, pleasant face glowed. [919] \"Is it from Scotland Yard?\" she gasped, \"I wouldn't have left home if I'd known.\" [920] \"We haven't notified Scotland Yard yet, Miss Muggleton. I'm a superintendent from Milchester.\" [921] \"Oh, I'm sure that's all right,\" Miss Mulgrew said vaguely. \"Have you found any clues?\" [922] \"What were you doing at the time of the crime, Miss Mulgrew?\" Miss Inchcliff asked, and winked at Cordeux. [923] \"Oh, my goodness,\" Miss Mulgrew gasped. \"Of course, I should have been prepared. Alibi, of course. Let me think\u2026 I was with everyone.\" [924] \"You weren't with me,\" Miss Inchcliff said. [925] \"Oh, my goodness, was I not? Yes, of course not. I was admiring the chrysanthemums. Such a pitiful species, really. Then everything happened \u2013 I mean, I didn't realize what was happening \u2013 my meaning is, I didn't know such a thing would happen. I didn't even think the revolver was real \u2013 everything was so awkward in the dark, and that dreadful scream. I was completely mistaken, you know. I thought she was being murdered \u2013 I mean, the refugee girl. I thought she was being cut her throat in the hall. I didn't know it was him \u2013 I mean, I didn't even know there was a man. I only heard one voice, you know, saying 'Please raise your hands.'\" [926] \"Hands up!\" Miss Chinchcliff corrected, \"There was no 'please' about it.\" [927] \"I was actually quite enjoying myself until the girl started screaming, and now that I think about it, it's quite horrifying. Being plunged into darkness was bad enough, and I felt hurt, terribly hurt. What else do you want to know, Inspector?\" [928] \"Nothing else,\" Inspector Coradine said thoughtfully, looking at Miss Mulgrew, \"I believe that's all.\" [929] Her friend laughed shortly and sharply. [930] \"He got you good, Miss Mulgrew,\" Coradine said. [931] \"I believe, Miss Quincannon,\" Miss Mulgrew replied, \"that if I knew, I would tell you everything.\" [932] \"That's not what he wants to hear,\" Miss Quincannon interjected. [933] She looked at the inspector. \"If you're going to visit people in their homes, I think you'll want to see the vicar next. You might learn something from her. Mrs. Harmon may seem a bit muddled \u2013 but I occasionally find her quite astute. Anyway, she knows some things.\" [934] As the inspector and Inspector Fletcher strode away, Amy Mulgrew suddenly gasped, \"Oh, Miss Quincannon, have I done terribly? I was so flustered!\" [935] \"Not at all,\" Miss Quincannon smiled, \"Overall, I must say you did quite well.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[128] \"You're still like a little girl, Round Round.\" [129] Reverend Julian Harmon, at the age of sixty, was a fine specimen of nature's creation, as he looked twenty-five years younger than he actually was. [130] \"I know I'm silly.\" [131] \"You're not silly, Roundy, you're clever.\" [132] \"No, I'm not clever, I'm not at all smart, even though I try... I like it when you read to me, tell me history and other things. And in the evenings, reading Gibbon's works to me seems a bit foolish. Because some things in Gibbon's books make you want to fall asleep when it's cold outside and the fire is warm and cozy.\" [133] Julian laughed. [134] \"But I do like listening to you read, Julian. Tell me that old parson's story about Ahasuerus again.\" [135] \"You can recite it, Roundy.\" [136] \"Tell