ing in a loud but slightly off-key voice:\n[148] It's a fine day to kill time,\n[149] Like a warm May day,\n[150] With the town's police nowhere to be found. [151] The sound of clattering dishes being placed in the sink drowned out the next line. But as Julien Harmon left the house, he heard the final triumphant and daring line of the song:\n[152] Murder's on the scene today. [153] * * *\n[154] [1] A nickname for Wuthering Heights. [155] [2] Confirmation: a Christian ceremony in which a person who has been baptized is confirmed as a member of the church. According to Christian doctrine, a child must be baptized and confirmed before they can become a full member of the church. [156] Edward Gibbon (1737-1794), a British historian. [157] A section of Scripture read during the morning prayer service in the Church of England. [158] Chapter 2: Breakfast Mystery\n[159] 1\n[160] In the small courtyard, breakfast was also being served. [161] Miss Blacklock was the mistress of the house, over sixty years old, sitting at the head of the table. She wore a country-style tweed suit and a short necklace made of large imitation pearls, which looked a bit out of place with her outfit. She was reading the section of the Daily Mail about the events on Necot Street. Julie Simmons was browsing the Telegraph in a lackluster manner, Patrick Simmons was examining the crossword puzzle in the Times, and Miss Dora Bunner was immersed in the local weekly newspaper. [162] Miss Blacklock was chuckling to herself. Meanwhile, Patrick grumbled, \"It's 'sticking' instead of 'adhesive' [1] \u2013 I got caught on that word.\" [163] Suddenly, there was a loud \"cluck\" from Miss Bunner's side. [164] \"Lettie [2] \u2013 Lettie \u2013 did you see this? What does this mean?\" [165] \"What's the matter, Dora?\" [166] \"An unusual notice, clearly inviting everyone to the small park. But what does it mean?\" [167] \"Let me see, dear Miss Bonner -\"\n[168] Miss Bonner handed the newspaper to Miss Blacklock, who reached out and took it, then pointed to the notice with her index finger. [169] \"Look here, Lettie.\" [170] Miss Blacklock looked at it, raised her eyebrows, and then loudly read out the notice. [171] \"A murder will take place at Little Park on Friday, October 29th at 6:30 p.m. Please be informed. Friends are requested to attend, no further notice will be given.\" [172] \"Patrick, is this your doing?\" she asked sharply, her gaze falling on the young man at the other end of the table, whose handsome face was well-known to all. [173] Patrick Simmons immediately denied it. [174] \"No, it's not, Aunt Letty. How could you think that? Why should I know about it?\" [175] \"Because you're the kind of person who would do something like that,\u201d Miss Blacklock said acerbically, \"to pull a prank or something.\" [176] \"Prank? Not a chance.\" [177] \"You, Julia?\" [178] \"Of course not,\" Julia answered listlessly. [179] \"You think, Mrs. Hyde-Miller...\" Miss Bonner trailed off, glancing at the empty seat at the table. [180] \"I don't think our Philippa would attempt such a whimsical thing,\" Patrick interjected. \"She's a serious girl, really.\" [181] \"But what is the purpose?\" Julia yawned, \"What does it mean?\" [182] Miss Blacklock spoke slowly, \"I suppose... it's some kind of silly trick.\" [183] \"But why?\" Miss Bonner exclaimed, \"What's the point? It looks like a silly joke, and very poor taste.\" [184] Her slack cheeks trembled with anger, and her myopic eyes flashed with fury. [185] Miss Blacklock smiled at her. [186] \"Don't worry about it, Bonnie,\" she said. \"It's just some guy's idea of a joke, but I'd like to know who's behind it.\" [187] \"It says tonight,\" Miss Bunner pointed out, \"Six thirty tonight. What do you think will happen?\" [188] \"Death!\" Patrick said grimly, \"The Death of Delight.\" [189] Miss Bunner gave a little shriek. \"Shut up, Patrick,\" Miss Blacklock said. [190] \"I was only referring to the special cake that Micky makes