ad rushed to Miss Bunner's aid, bringing her back to settle in the country, and concocting excuses to comfort her: \"There are too many chores for me to handle alone, so I need someone to help me manage the household.\" However, before long - the doctor had warned her - she would often feel that bringing Miss Bunner was a mistake. Miss Bunner made a mess of everything, causing the eccentric foreign \"helper\" to become agitated; she would count the laundry wrong, misplace bills and letters, and sometimes irritate the capable Miss Blacklock to the point of anger and pain. However, poor and confused Miss Bunner was so loyal and eager to help, feeling so happy and proud to be able to do something for others - unfortunately, she was utterly unreliable. [202] Miss Blacklock spoke sharply. [203] \"Don't do that, Miss Bunner. You know I told you -\"\n[204] \"Oh,\" Miss Bunner said apologetically, \"I know. I forgot, but - but you're worried, aren't you?\" [205] \"Worried? No,\" she added sincerely, \"at least, not very. Are you talking about the silly advertisement in The Times?\" [206] \"Yes. Even if it's a joke, it seems - it seems cruel.\" [207] \"Cruel?\" [208] \"Yes. I just feel that there's something a bit cruel about it. I mean - it's not a nice joke.\" [209] Miss Blacklock looked at her friend, regarding the soft eyes, long and obstinate mouth, the slightly upturned nose. Poor Dora, so caught up in her own world, so muddled, so involved, so irritating. A lovable and over-sensitive old fool, but strangely intuitive. [210] \"I think you're right, Dora,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"It's not a nice joke.\" [211] \"I don't like it at all,\" Miss Dora Bonner said in a tone of unusual firmness, \"It frightens me.\" [212] \"Nonsense,\" Miss Blacklock retorted sharply. [213] \"It's dangerous, that's what I feel about it, just as if someone had put a bomb in a parcel and sent it to you.\" [214] \"Darling, it's just some silly fool trying to play a trick.\" [215] \"But it's not funny.\" [216] Indeed it was... Miss Blacklock's expression gave away her thoughts, and Miss Bunner triumphantly exclaimed: \"There, you think so yourself!\u201d\n[217] \"But, Miss Bunner, my dear...\u201d\n[218] She broke off. A young woman, her bosom swelling with emotion, her figure wrapped in a tight knit sweater, her lower half clad in a dazzlingly bright skirt, her oily dark hair coiled atop her head, dark eyes glowing, burst in at the door. [219] \"Can I speak to you? Can I, may I not, please?\u201d She fired off her questions like a machine gun. [220] Miss Blacklock sighed. [221] \"Of course, Mickey, what's the matter?\" [222] Sometimes she thought that it would be easier to do all the household chores and cooking herself rather than dealing with this refugee \"helper\" who was constantly moody. [223] \"I'm going to tell you - is that the right order? I'm going to notify you, I'm leaving - I'm leaving right now!\" [224] \"Why? Has someone disturbed you?\" [225] \"Yes, I'm very upset,\" Mickey said dramatically, \"I don't want to die! I've escaped from the continent of Europe. My family is dead - all killed - my mother, little brother, and dear little niece - all, all killed. But I escaped - I hid. I came to England. I work. I do things that I would never do in my own country - I -\"\n[226] \"I understand all that, Miss Blacklock,\" said the woman firmly. These words were always on Mickey's lips. \"But why do you want to leave now?\" [227] \"Because they're coming to kill me!\" [228] \"Who's coming to kill you?\" [229] \"My enemies. Nazis! Maybe this time it's the Bolsheviks. They found me here, they're coming to kill me. I saw it \u2013 yes \u2013 in the newspaper!\" [230] \"Oh, you mean in The News?\" [231] \"Here, it's right here.\" Midge took the newspaper she had been hiding behind and showed it to her. \"See\u2014it says there's a murder, right here in the park. That's here, isn't it? At six thirty tonight. Ah! I don't want to wait to be killed\u2014no, I don't!\" [232] \"But why should this be about you? This is\u2014we think it's a joke.\" [233] \"A joke? Murder isn't a joke, is it?\" [234] \"No, of course not. But, my dear child, if someone wanted to kill you, they wouldn't advertise it in the newspaper, would they?\" [235] \"You don't think they would?\" Midge seemed to shiver. \"You don't think, perhaps, they don't really intend to kill anybody? Maybe they're after you, Miss Blacklock.\" [236] \"I certainly don't believe anyone wants to harm me.\" Miss Blacklock replied lightly, \"And honestly, Miki, I can't see why anyone would want to harm you. After all, what reason would they have?\" [237] \"Because they're all bad... very, very bad people. I tell you, my mother, my little brother, my adorable niece\u2026\"\n[238] \"Yes, that's true,\" Miss Blacklock quickly cut her off, \"But I really can't believe anyone would want to harm you, Miki. Of course, if you want to leave, I can't stop you. But I think it would be unwise if you did.\" [239] As Miki hesitated, Miss Blacklock added decisively, \"Let's cook the beef that the butcher brought over for lunch. That meat looks tough.\" [240] \"I'll make you a stewed beef, a secret recipe.\" [241] \"If you want to call that dish that, of course. And perhaps you should use up that hard cheese to make some cheese straws. I think someone might come by tonight for a drink.\" [242] \"Tonight? What do you mean, tonight?\" [243] \"Six thirty.\" [244] \"But that's the time they said it was in the paper. Why come then? Why do they want to come?\" [245] \"They're coming for the funeral,\" Miss Blacklock's eyes sparkled, \"Well, that's all, Mickey. I'm busy. Go out and close the door when you leave.\" She said firmly. [246] \"She should be able to calm down for a while.\" The door closed with a suspicious look on Midge's face, she concluded. [247] \"You're really efficient, Letty,\" Miss Bonner said admiringly. [248] * *\n[249] The former is spelled \"adherent,\" and the latter is \"adhesive.\" [250] \"Lettie\" is a nickname for Letticia. [251] [3] A nickname for Bonner. [252] Chapter 3: Six O'Clock Half Past\n[253] 1\n[254] \"All right, everything is ready.\" Miss Blacklock said. She surveyed the drawing room with a critical eye: a rose-patterned tablecloth on the table against the wall, two bronze-colored chrysanthemums in vases, violets in small vases, and a silver cigarette case on the center table. The tray with drinks was on the table. [255] The small park is a house with an early Victorian style, of medium size, with a long shaded gallery and several green shutters. The narrow drawing room was intercepted by the roof of the gallery. At one end of the room, there were two doors leading to a small room with a bay window. The previous occupants had removed the two doors and replaced them with velvet drapes. Miss Blacklock removed the drapes, merging the two rooms into one. There were two fireplaces at each end of the drawing room, but neither was lit, yet the room was still warm. [256] \"You turned on the central heating?\" Patrick asked. [257] Miss Blacklock nodded. [258] \"It's been misty and damp lately, and the whole house feels damp. I had Evans turn it on before he left.\" [259] \"You used the very, very precious coal dust?\" Patrick asked sarcastically. [260] \"As you said, yes, precious coal dust, otherwise we would have had to use even more precious coal. You know, the fuel office doesn't even give us our weekly allowance unless we can prove we have no other means of cooking.\" [261] \"I suppose everyone had a big pile of coal back then?\" Julia asked with interest, as if hearing a fairy tale. [262] \"Yes, and it was cheap.\" [263] \"Anyone could buy it, and as much as they wanted, without filling out any forms, and there weren't any shortages, were there? Plenty of coal?\" [264] \"And it was of all kinds and qualities \u2013 unlike the coal now, which is all slag.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1702] \"And I've always thought,\" she said, \"that being a man must be terribly boring.\" [1703] Then she said thoughtfully, \"I think Randall mainly saw Blanche as a brother. He relied on her judgment, and her judgment was always so good. You know, she's helped him out of difficulties more than once.\" [1704] \"She told me she saved him once with her money?\" [1705] \"Yes, but I mean more than that. After all these years, I can tell the truth. Randall can't tell right from wrong, he's slow to feel, this poor dear doesn't know what cunning or deceit is. Blanche kept him from going astray. Letitia Blacklock has a quality that is absolutely upright. She would never do anything dishonest. Her character is excellent, you know. I've always admired her. They had a hard life when they were girls. Their father was a country doctor - slow-witted and obstinate - a tyrant in the house. Letitia ran away to England and trained to be a certified accountant. Her sister is somewhat disabled, probably deformed somewhere, so she never sees anyone and stays at home. So, when the old man died, Letitia gave up everything and went home to take care of her sister. Randall was angry with her - but it didn't matter. Once Letitia decides what is her responsibility, she will not be swayed, no matter how you argue with her.\" [1706] \"How long before your husband died?\" [1707] \"Two or three years, I think. Randall left his will before he died, and it hasn't been changed. He said to me, 'We have no children.' (Our little boy, you know, died when he was two.) 'After we're gone, it would be best for Blanche to take the money. She'll make a name for herself, you'll see.'\" [1708] \"You see,\" Bella continued, \"Randall enjoyed making money - not just having it - but the excitement, the risks, and the thrill. Blanche liked all that too. She has the same adventurous spirit and the same decisiveness. Poor thing, she's never experienced those ordinary pleasures - falling in love, leading a man around by the nose, testing them - building a family, raising children, enjoying the real pleasures of life.\" [1709] Kradok was surprised: a woman who had suffered from chronic illness her whole life, her only child had died, and her husband had died. She lived a lonely widowhood and had been a hopeless invalid for years. Yet she had genuine compassion for others and scorned pain. [1710] She nodded at him. [1711] \"I know what you're thinking. But I have everything that makes life worth living - I may have lost it all - but I once had it. I was pretty and happy when I was a girl, I married the man I loved, and he never stopped loving me... As for children, he died, but we spent two precious years together... I've suffered a lot physically - but because of the pain, you learn to appreciate the wonderful joy that comes when the pain stops. Moreover, everyone has always been good to me... I am a lucky woman, really.\" [1712] Cora Dedalus found an opening in what she had just said. [1713] \"Ms. Goddard, you said that your husband left the money to Miss Blacklock because he had no other heirs. But that's not entirely true, is it? He had a sister.\" [1714] \"Ah, Sonya. They had a falling out years ago and have been estranged ever since.\" [1715] \"He didn't agree with her marriage?\" [1716] \"Yes, she married a man - what was his name -\"\n[1717] \"Stanfordtis.\" [1718] \"That's him, Dimetri Stanfordtis. Randall always thought he was a fraud. The two men never liked each other from the start. But Sonya was madly in love with him and wanted to marry him. I couldn't understand why she couldn't just marry him. Men's views on such things are always so strange. Sonya was no longer a little girl - she was twenty-five, and she knew what she was doing. He was a fraud, I'm sure - I mean, he was a genuine fraud. I believe he had a criminal record - Randall always suspected he wasn't using his real name. All of this Sonya knew. The problem was - Randall couldn't agree - that Dimetri was a man who was extremely attractive to women, and he loved Sonya just as much as she loved him. Randall insisted he married her for the money - but that wasn't the case. Sonya was very pretty, you know, and ambitious. If the marriage didn't work out, if Dimetri didn't treat her well or was unfaithful, she would have left to minimize the loss. She was a wealthy woman who could live as she pleased.\" [1719] \"Did their rift ever resolve?\" [1720] \"No. Randall and Sonya never got along well. She hated him for trying to stop the marriage. She said, 'Well, you're so unreasonable! I'll never speak to you again!'\" [1721] \"But it wasn't like that for you, was it?\" [1722] Bella smiled. \"Eighteen months later, I received a letter from her. I remember it was from Budapest, but she didn't leave an address. She told me to tell Randall that she was extremely happy and had a pair of twins.\" [1723] \"Did she tell you their names?\" [1724] Bella smiled again. \"She said they were born just after noon \u2014 she planned to name them Pip and Emma [2]. Of course, these names could have been a joke.\" [1725] \"Did you hear from her again after that?\" [1726] \"No. She said she and her husband were going to take their little ones to America for a while. And then I never heard from her again\u2026\"\n[1727] \"Do you happen to still have that letter?\" [1728] \"No, I'm afraid I don't. I told Randall about the letter, and he just grumbled, 'There's always going to be a day when she regrets marrying that guy.' That's all he said about it. We had actually forgotten about her. She was out of our lives\u2026\"\n[1729] \"But Mr. Goddard left his property to her children in case Miss Blacklock passed away before you?\" [1730] \"Oh, that was my idea. When he told me about the will, I said, 'What if I die before her?' He was surprised. I said, 'Well, I know Blacklock is as strong as a horse, and I'm a delicate flower - but you know, accidents can happen, and squeaky doors last longer.' Then he said, 'There's no one else - none at all.' So I said, 'Well, there's Sonya.' He immediately retorted, 'Let that guy have my money? No way!' I said, 'Well, then give it to her children. Pip and Emma, and there might be more by now.' So he grumbled and grumbled, but he added that in.\" [1731] \"Since then,\" Corrodick said slowly, \"you haven't heard anything about your sister-in-law and her children?\" [1732] \"No \u2013 they may be dead, or \u2013 anywhere.\" [1733] They might be in Chipping Cheddar, Corrodick thought. [1734] Bella Goddard seemed to read his mind, and her eyes widened in surprise. She said, \"Don't let them hurt Blanche. Blanche is a good person \u2013 very good \u2013 you must stop them from hurting her \u2013\"\n[1735] Her voice suddenly cut off. Corrodick saw a gray shadow appear in the corners of her mouth and eyes. [1736] \"You are tired,\" he said, \"I must leave.\" [1737] She nodded. [1738] \"Call Mac[3] in,\" she whispered, \"Yes...I am tired...\"\n[1739] Her hand weakly moved. \"Take care of Blairch...never let anything happen to her...take care of her...\"\n[1740] \"I will do my best, Mrs. Goodlady.\" He stood up and walked towards the door. [1741] Her voice was so faint it was like a thread, coming from behind him...\n[1742] \"Time is short... Before I die... She is in danger... Take care of her...\"\n[1743] As he went out, the MacLane nurse passed him and he spoke uneasily, \"I hope I haven't caused her any harm.\" [1744] \"Oh, I don't think so, Mr. Corrado,\" she said, \"I told you she would suddenly tire.\" [1745] Later, he asked the nurse, \"There's just one thing I didn't get to ask Mrs. Goddard... Do you happen to have any photos of her from the past? If so, I'd like to...\"\n[1746] She interrupted him. [1747] \"I'm afraid there's nothing like that. All her personal documents and belongings were kept in the London mansion during the early days of the war. Mrs. Goddard was very ill at the time. Everything saved there was destroyed during the Blitz. Mrs. Goddard was very upset about losing so many personal mementos and family documents. I'm afraid there's nothing left here.\" [1748] So that's that, thought Corrado. [1749] However, he felt the trip was not in vain. The ghosts of Pip and Emma, the twin siblings, were not mere figments. [1750] Corrado thought. \"There are a pair of siblings raised in Europe. Mrs. Goddard was a wealthy woman when she married, but money dispersed quickly in Europe at that time. During the war, economic fluctuations were very unusual. These two young people, the children of the man with the criminal record, were left almost penniless in England. What would they do? Look for the whereabouts of all their wealthy relatives. Their uncle, a wealthy millionaire, had passed away. So the first thing they would do is look for the will to see if they or their mother happened to inherit the money. They went to the law firm and learned the contents of the will. Then they may have also learned about Miss Letitia Blacklock, who is still alive. They inquired about the situation of Mrs. Randall Goddard, the widow. She was a sick woman living in Scotland and they learned she was not long for this world. If Miss Letitia Blacklock died before her, they would inherit a large fortune. So what would they do next?\" [1751] Cora Dedek continued to ponder: \"They won't go to Scotland. They'll find out where Miss Letitia Blacklock is living now and then go there \u2013 but not under their real identities\u2026 They'll go together \u2013 or separately? Emma\u2026 I really want to know\u2026 Pip and Emma, if either of them isn't in Chipping Cleghorn, I'll eat my hat\u2026\"\n[1752] * * *\n[1753] [1] The nickname of Blacklock. [1754] [2] Pip and Emma, when put together, mean \"afternoon\" in English. [1755] [3] The abbreviation of McLean. [1756] Chapter 15: The Death of a Delicacy\n[1757]1\n[1758] In the kitchen of the small estate, Miss Blacklock was giving instructions to Midge. [1759] \"Tomato sandwiches and sardine sandwiches, and your excellent scones, and I want you to make your specialty cake.\" [1760] \"You want so much, is it a gathering?\" [1761] \"It's Miss Bonner's birthday, some people are coming for tea.\" [1762] \"At her age, people don't celebrate birthdays, it's best to forget.\" [1763] \"But she doesn't want to forget! Some people are going to give her presents \u2013 so we're having a little party, it will be nice.\" [1764] \"You said the same last time \u2013 and look what happened!\" [1765] Miss Blacklock managed to restrain herself from reacting. [1766] \"Well, there won't be anything this time.\" [1767] \"How do you know what will happen in this house? I'm always trembling, and at night I lock the door and look into the wardrobe to see if anyone is hiding in there.\" [1768] \"That will certainly make you feel better and safer,\" Miss Blacklock said coldly. [1769] \"The cake you want me to make \u2014 is it the kind \u2014\" Midge uttered a sound that, to Miss Blacklock's ears accustomed to English, sounded like \"sweating\" in German, or like cats spitting at each other. [1770] \"Yes, that kind. It's quite heavy.\" [1771] \"Well, it's rather greasy. But I have nothing to make such a cake. I need chocolate, lots of cream, sugar, and raisins.\" [1772] \"You can use the can of cream that was sent from America, and the raisins we were going to save for Christmas, there's a thick piece of chocolate and a pound of sugar here.\" [1773] Midge's face lit up with a radiant smile. [1774] \"So, on your behalf, I'll do it,\" she exclaimed happily, \"it will be sweet and creamy, melt-in-your-mouth! I'll put chocolate frosting on top - I'll do a good job, and I'll write good wishes on it. These British cakes taste like sand, they've never even tasted such a cake. Delicious, they'll say - oh, so delicious!\" [1775] Her face clouded over again. [1776] \"Mr. Patrick called it the 'death of deliciousness.' My cake! I don't want anyone to call it that!\" [1777] \"He was actually complimenting you,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"He meant that it's so good, it's worth dying for.\" [1778] Micky looked at her skeptically. [1779] \"But I don't like the word 'die.' They won't die because of my cake, no, they'll feel very, very good\u2026\"\n[1780] \"I'm sure we will.\" [1781] Miss Blacklock turned away from the kitchen, relieved that the negotiations had gone well. Mickey was impossible to predict. [1782] She met Dora Bunner in the hallway outside the kitchen. [1783] \"Oh, Letty, shall I go in and tell Mickey how to cut the sandwiches?\" [1784] \"Don't go,\" said Miss Blacklock, firmly leading her friend into the hallway, \"she's in a good mood now, and I don't want to disturb her.\" [1785] \"But I can teach her how to\u2014\"\n[1786] \"Please don't teach her anything, Dora. These Central Europeans don't like being told what to do, they hate it.\" [1787] Dora looked at her inquiringly and then suddenly smiled. [1788] \"Edmund Swithinham just phoned. He wished me a happy birthday and said he'd bring a jar of honey as a present this afternoon. How kind, isn't it? I can't imagine how he would know it's my birthday.\" [1789] \"It seems everyone knows. You must have been talking about it, Dora.\" [1790] \"Oh, I just happened to mention that I turned fifty-nine today\u2014\"\n[1791] \"You're sixty-four,\" Miss Blacklock's eyes sparkled with amusement. [1792] \"But Miss Quincannon said, 'You don't look your age. How old do you guess I am?' That was a very awkward question, because Miss Quincannon looks so odd, she could be any age. She said she'd bring some eggs along for me. I told her our hens haven't been laying many eggs lately.\" [1793] \"You've done quite well for a birthday, Dora,\" Miss Blacklock said, \"honey, eggs\u2014and Julia brought a whole box of chocolates\u2014\"\n[1794] \"I have no idea where she got that stuff.\" [1795] \"Best not to ask. Her methods are, strictly speaking, illegal.\" [1796] \"And your lovely brooch,\" said Miss Bunn, lowering her head and looking proudly at the small diamond leaf pinned to her chest. [1797] \"Do you like it? I'm glad. I never liked jewellery.\" [1798] \"I do like it.\" [1799] \"Good. Let's go and feed the ducks.\" [1800] 2\n[1801] \"Ha!\" Patrick called out solemnly as the birthday dinner began around the dining table, \"What is this before me? Delicious death.\" [1802] \"Shh,\" Miss Blacklock hushed, \"Don't let Midge hear you call her cake that. She'll be offended.\" [1803] \"But it is delicious death! This is Bonnie's birthday cake?\" [1804] \"Yes,\" Miss Bunn said, \"I'm enjoying the most delightful birthday.\" [1805] Her cheeks flushed with excitement. After Captain Eastbrook presented her with a box of candy, bowed, and said, \"Sweet sugar for a sweetheart!\" she had been like this ever since. [1806] When the colonel paid her a compliment, Julia turned her face away sharply, causing Miss Blacklock to frown. [1807] After the main course was finished, they came around with a round of cookies, and then everyone stood up from their seats. [1808] \"I feel a little unwell,\" Julia said, \"It must be because of the cake. I remember feeling the same last time.\" [1809] \"It's worth it,\" Patrick said. [1810] \"These foreigners certainly know their way around pastries,\" Miss Chinchcliff said, \"They just don't know how to make a proper boiled pudding.\" [1811] Everyone respected and didn't express their opinions, although Patrick had a sentence on his lips, wondering if anyone really wanted to eat a plain steamed pudding. [1812] \"Did you hire a new gardener?\" Miss Chickchaff asked Miss Blacklock when they returned to the living room. [1813] \"No, what's the matter?\" [1814] \"I saw a man peering around the chicken coop. He looked very proud and like a soldier.\" [1815] \"Oh, that's our detective,\" Julia said. [1816] Mrs. Eastbrook dropped her handbag upon hearing this. [1817] \"Detective?\" she exclaimed. \"But\u2014but\u2014why?\" [1818] \"I don't know,\" Julia said. \"He was walking around, staring at the house. I suppose he was protecting Aunt Letty.\" [1819] \"Nonsense,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"I can take care of myself, thank you.\" [1820] \"Although the matter is probably over,\" Mrs. Eastbrook called out. \"But I still want to ask, why did they stop questioning?\" [1821] \"The police weren't satisfied,\" her husband replied, \"That's all.\" [1822] \"But what weren't they satisfied with?\" [1823] Colonel Eastbrook shook his head, with an air of knowing more than he was letting on.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nMiss Marple turned her head and saw Miss Blacklock standing behind them\u2014she must have just come in. [1597] \"Coffee and gossip, Dora?\" said Miss Blacklock, with a reproachful tone. \"Good morning, Miss Marple. It's so cold, isn't it?\" [1598] \"We were just talking,\" said Miss Bunner hastily, \"there are so many rules and regulations now that it's hard to keep track of them all.\" [1599] The door burst open, and Round swung into \"The Bluebird.\" [1600] \"Hello,\" she called, \"did I miss the coffee?\" [1601] \"No, dear,\" said Miss Marple. \"Sit down and have some.\" [1602] \"We must be going home,\" said Miss Blacklock. \"Have you finished shopping, Bonnie?\" [1603] Her voice was once again filled with condescension, but her eyes still held a hint of reproach. [1604] \"Yes, yes, thank you, Letty,\" said Miss Bunce. \"I need to stop by the pharmacy for some aspirin and a blister plaster.\" [1605] After the door to \"The Bluebird\" closed behind them, Round asked, \"What were you talking about?\" [1606] Miss Marple didn't answer right away. After she had finished her tea, she said, \"Family ties are a very powerful thing. Very powerful. Do you remember that famous case? I can't remember which one it was. They said the husband poisoned the wife, and put the poison in a glass of wine. At the trial, the daughter said she drank half of her mother's glass - which negated the charge against the father. They did say - though perhaps it was just a rumor - that after that, she never spoke to her father again and never lived with him again. Of course, a father is one thing, but a nephew or cousin is another. But it's still the same - no one wants their family to be hanged, right?\" [1607] \"Yes,\" Round thought for a moment. \"I don't think they would.\" [1608] Miss Marple leaned back in her chair and murmured to herself, \"People are so alike, no matter where they are.\" [1609] \"Who do I look like?\" [1610] \"Well, dear, to be honest, you look like yourself. I can't think of anyone else you remind me of, except perhaps -\"\n[1611] \"You're doing it again,\" said Round Round. [1612] \"I was just thinking of one of my former parlour maids, dear.\" [1613] \"I'd be a terrible maid,\" said Round Round. [1614] \"Yes, dear, she was too. She wasn't very good at serving others at the table. The table was always a mess, the kitchen knives mixed with the dining room knives, and her hat - this was a long time ago - she never wore it properly.\" [1615] Round Round unconsciously adjusted her hat. [1616] \"What happened next?\" she asked eagerly. [1617] \"I kept her on because she was such good company and always made me laugh. I liked her straightforward way of talking. One day she said to me, 'Of course, I don't know, ma'am,' she said, 'but Miss Florence's posture is just like a married woman.' Poor Miss Florence did have trouble \u2013 she fell in love with a gentleman from the hair salon who was very polite. I spoke to him, and they had a very nice wedding and settled down happily. Miss Florence was a good girl, but she was easily attracted to polite men.\" [1618] \"Did she do anything criminal?\" asked Yuan Yuan. \"I mean, the parlor maid.\" [1619] \"No, really,\" said Miss Marple. \"She married a Baptist minister, and they had three children.\" [1620] \"Just like me,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"Although so far, I only have Edward and Susan.\" [1621] After a moment, she added, \"Who are you thinking of, Aunt Jane?\" [1622] \"Many people, dear, many people,\" Miss Marple said evasively. [1623] \"In St. Mary Mead?\" [1624] \"Mostly, I suppose... I thought of Nurse Eaton \u2013 a truly remarkable, kind woman. She took care of an elderly lady who seemed to really like her. Then the lady died. And then another lady, and she died too. It turned out she had been administering morphine. In the kindest way possible, it was truly horrifying, that the woman didn't even feel she had done wrong. 'They weren't going to live long anyway,' she said. One had cancer, quite painful.\" [1625] \"You mean \u2013 it was a merciful murder?\" [1626] \"No, no. They left her money in their wills. She did it for the money, you know...\"\n[1627] \"Then there was the young man on the liner\u2014Pussycat's nephew from the paper shop. He brought home things he had stolen, said he bought them abroad, and she believed him. Then the police came and questioned him, and he pinned it all on her, so she couldn't get out of it... He wasn't a good man\u2014but he was good-looking, and two women fell in love with him. He spent a lot of money on one of them.\" [1628] \"I think that was the dirtiest one,\" said Round Round. [1629] \"Yes, dear. And then there was Mrs. Crewe from the wool shop, who doted on her son and spoiled him rotten. He got mixed up with a bad crowd. Do you remember Joan Crowfoot, Round Round?\" [1630] \"No, I don't.\" [1631] \"I think you met her when I took you around, always with a cigarette or a pipe in her mouth, striding around. There was a bank robbery, and Joan Crowfoot was there at the time. She knocked the man down and took his revolver. The judge commended her bravery. [1632] Round listened intently, as if committing everything to memory. [1633] \"And?\" she asked. [1634] \"That summer, there was a girl from Saint-Jean-de-Collins \u2013 such a quiet girl \u2013 not quiet in the sense of being silent, but everyone liked her, but not very well. We heard her husband was a forger, which made her feel isolated by people. It changed her, you know, depression can change a person. [1635] \"Do you remember any British colonels who served in India, dear?\" [1636] \"Of course, dear. There's a Major Vance at Rajes, and a Colonel Wright in Shimla. They're fine. But I do remember Mr. Hodgson, who went on a long journey and married a young woman who could have been his daughter. I don't know where she came from \u2013 except what she told him.\" [1637] \"And she wasn't telling the truth?\" [1638] \"No, dear, she definitely wasn't.\" [1639] \"Not bad,\" Round nodded, counting people on her fingers, \"we have the whole-hearted Dorla, the dashing Patrick, Mrs. Sweetnam, Edmund, Philippa Hemmes, Colonel Eastbrook and his wife \u2013 if I may say so, your opinion about Dorla is spot on. But she had no reason to murder Letitia Blacklock.\" [1640] \"There may be something Letitia Blacklock knew but didn't want others to know.\" [1641] \"Oh, dear, just the usual old stuff? That must be ancient history.\" [1642] \"Perhaps not. You see, Round Round, you're not the type to care what people think of you.\" [1643] \"I see what you mean,\" Round Round said suddenly. \"When you've been through a lot of hard times, like a lost cat shaking with cold, and then you find a home, a warm hand to stroke you, and people call you a pretty kitty and care for you wholeheartedly\u2026 to keep all that, you'd do anything. Well, I must say, you've shown me all sorts of people.\" [1644] \"But you don't see them clearly.\" Miss Marple said gently. [1645] \"Do I miss anything? Julia? Julia, the lovely Julia is so odd.\" [1646] \"Three shillings and sixpence,\" the waitress said, coming out of the shadows. [1647] \"And besides,\" she went on, her chest heaving under the \"bluebird\" on her uniform, \"I want to know, Mrs. Harmon, why you say I'm peculiar. I have a cousin who's one of the 'peculiar ones', but I've always been a Church of England myself, and retired Hopkins can tell you that.\" [1648] \"I'm really sorry,\" round round said. \"I was just quoting a song. I didn't mean you. I didn't know your name was Julia.\" [1649] \"Well, that's quite a coincidence,\" the waitress said, her attitude softening. \"I believe you didn't mean to offend, but hearing my name\u2026 well, it's natural, isn't it? If you think someone is talking about you, it's human nature to listen. Thank you.\" [1650] She took the tip and left. [1651] \"But it must be,\" Miss Marple murmured, \"it can't be like this. It doesn't make sense -\"\n[1652] \"Auntie!\" said Roundhay. [1653] Miss Marple sighed and smiled brightly. [1654] \"Nothing, dear,\" she said. [1655] \"Do you think you know who the murderer is?\" asked Roundhay. \"Who is it?\" [1656] \"I haven't the slightest idea,\" replied Miss Marple. \"I suddenly had an idea\u2014but it disappeared. I wish I knew. Time is so short, so very short.\" [1657] \"What do you mean by short?\" [1658] \"The Scottish old lady could die at any moment.\" [1659] Yuan Yuan's eyes widened. \"So you really believe in Pip and Emma, then? You think they did it\u2014and they'll do it again?\" [1660] \"They'll certainly do it again,\" said Miss Marple almost absent-mindedly. \"Once you've made up your mind to kill someone, you don't give up because you've failed the first time. Especially when you're sure you haven't been suspected.\" [1661] \"But if it's Pip and Emma,\" said Round Round, \"there are only two possibilities. It must be Patrick and Julia. They're brother and sister, and their ages fit.\" [1662] \"It's not so simple, my dear. There are all sorts of possibilities and combinations. There's Pip's wife\u2014if he's married\u2014or Emma's husband. There's their mother\u2014even if she can't inherit directly, she's interested. If Miss Blacklock hasn't seen her for thirty years, she may not recognize her now. Older women all look alike. You remember Mrs. Waters, who collected her own pension and Mrs. Butler's as well, even though Mrs. Butler had been dead for years. And Miss Blacklock is myopic. Have you noticed how she looks at people? Then there's their father, who's obviously a bad lot.\" [1663] \"Yes, but he's a foreigner.\" [1664] \"That's true, but there's no reason to believe that he speaks English with an accent or that he gestures wildly when he talks. I dare say he's playing the part of\u2014a British army officer serving in India, and doing it just as well as anyone else.\" [1665] \"Is that your idea?\" [1666] \"No, not really, dear. I just thought that a large sum of money is in danger, a large sum indeed. I know only too well that people will do terrible things for a large sum of money.\" [1667] \"I think they would, but it wouldn't benefit them, would it? There would be repercussions?\" [1668] \"Yes\u2014but they usually don't think that way.\" [1669] \"I can understand that.\" The Roundhead suddenly smiled, a sweet, crooked smile. \"Everyone feels differently about money\u2026 even me. I've been thinking, 'You'll get that money, and you'll use it for all sorts of good things. Make plans\u2026 provide a home for abandoned children. Send hard-working women to a foreign country to rest and relax after a lifetime of toil\u2026'\"\n[1670] Her expression turned somber, and her eyes suddenly looked sad and melancholy. [1671] \"I know what you're thinking,\" she said to Miss Marple, \"you think I would be the worst kind of person because I have children. If it's just for selfish reasons that you want that money, you would feel ashamed. But once you pretend that you're using the money for good deeds, you can convince yourself that maybe killing isn't such a big deal...\"\n[1672] Then her eyes brightened again. [1673] \"But I can't do it,\" she said. \"I can't bring myself to do it. Not even to old people, sick people, or people who have done terrible things in the world. Even if it's someone who is cheating or \u2013 or a real beast, it's not okay.\" [1674] She blew gently on the fly. It moved its legs and then wobbled away. [1675] \"Cheer up, dear Aunt Jane,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"I would never kill anyone.\" [1676] Chapter 14: Looking Back\n[1677] After a night on the train, Inspector Cordiner alighted at a small station in the Scottish Highlands. [1678] For a moment, he was surprised that the wealthy Mrs. Goddard \u2014 a disabled woman \u2014 had chosen to live in the remote Scottish home instead of the fashionable square in London, the Hampshire estate, or the villa in the south of France. She must have cut herself off from many friends and entertainment. This must be a lonely life \u2014 or was she so sick that she couldn't notice or care about her surroundings anymore? [1679] A car waited to pick him up, an old-fashioned, spacious Daimler driven by an elderly driver. It was a sunny morning, and during the twenty-mile drive, the inspector enjoyed himself despite being surprised by this choice for seclusion. A casual remark opened the driver's floodgates, giving him a rough understanding of the reason behind the decision. [1680] \"It's her home before she got married. Oh, she's the last one of the family. She was happier here with Mr. Goddard than anywhere else, even though he couldn't often get away from London. But whenever he came, they were like two children. Happy.\" [1681] As the ancient mansion's gray walls came into view, Inspector Corrado felt as though time had reversed. An elderly male butler greeted him, and after freshening up, the inspector was led to a room with a roaring fireplace, where he had breakfast. [1682] After breakfast, a middle-aged nurse dressed in a nurse's uniform entered the room, introducing herself as McLean Nurse. [1683] \"My patient is ready to see you, Inspector Corrado. She's been looking forward to meeting you.\u201d\n[1684] \"I'll do my best not to upset her,\" Corrado promised. [1685] \"I should warn you about what to expect. You'll find Mrs. Goddard to be perfectly normal. She'll talk and enjoy talking, and then - suddenly - her energy will collapse. At that point, please leave immediately and call me. You'll see that she's mostly kept alive by morphine. Most of the time, she's in a half-conscious state. I've given her a shot of stimulant to stay awake for your visit. As the stimulant wears off, she'll return to her semi-comatose state.\" [1686] \"I understand, Nurse MacLane. Would it be appropriate for me to ask about Mrs. Goddard's exact health condition?\" [1687] \"Well, Mr. Corrado, she is a dying woman. Her life expectancy is only a few weeks. If you find it strange that she has not passed away years ago, you may be right, but that is the truth. What has kept Mrs. Goddard alive is her intense desire and love for life. For someone who has suffered for years and has not left her home in fifteen years, it may seem unusual, but it is true. Mrs. Goddard has never been a robust woman, yet her will to survive has been remarkable. She is still a very charming woman, you will find.\" [1688] Corrado was led into a large bedroom with a fire burning, where an elderly woman lay on a bed with a canopy. Although she was only a few years older than Letitia Blacklock, her frail body made her look much older. [1689] Her hair was neatly combed, and a light blue woolen wrap covered her neck and shoulders. The lines on her face expressed pain, but there was also sweetness. It was strange that her faded blue eyes sparkled with what Corrado could only describe as a mischievous gaze. [1690] \"Well, this is quite interesting,\" she said. \"I don't often have police visits. I hear that Letitia Blacklock was not seriously injured in that attack? How is my dear Blanche?\" [1691] \"She is fine, Mrs. Goddard. She sends her regards.\" [1692] \"I haven't seen her in a long time... Many years have passed since she returned to England, and I invited her to stay here, but she said that after so much time, meeting old acquaintances would be painful. Perhaps she was right... Blythe is a very wise woman. About a year ago, an old school friend of mine came to visit me, but, hmm!\" [1693] She smiled slightly. \"We looked at each other with disgust. After asking 'Do you remember?' there was nothing more to say. It was quite awkward.\" [1694] Mr. Coleridge willingly let her ramble on, hoping to gain insight into the supposed family atmosphere between the Goddards and Blacklock. [1695] \"I suppose,\" Beryl shrewdly asked, \"you want to know about the money? Randall left a will stating that the money should go to Blythe upon my death. Of course, Randall never thought I would outlive him. He was a big, strong man who never got sick; I was always complaining about aches and pains. Doctors came every few days, looking at my condition with a frown.\" [1696] \"I don't think 'complaining' is the right word, Mrs. Goddard.\" [1697] The old lady chuckled. [1698] \"I didn't mean complaining in the sense of whining. I never felt too sorry for myself. But being so weak, everyone naturally thought I would go first. But it wasn't that way. No, not at all.\" [1699] \"Exactly, why did your husband do that with his money?\" [1700] \"You mean why did he leave it to Blair? Not for the reasons you might imagine.\" [1701] Kroll looked at the frail old lady on the big bed with interest. He realized that Bella Goddard had always enjoyed and still enjoyed being a woman. She blinked at him.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."